00:00:09.990 --> 00:00:15.259
In the early 1980s, Britain began discussing
with China the future of its last remaining

00:00:15.259 --> 00:00:18.000
major colony, Hong Kong.

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:23.320
Immediately, Beijing made clear, in no uncertain
terms, that it would neither accept an independent

00:00:23.320 --> 00:00:27.660
Hong Kong, nor a Chinese one administered
by Britain.

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:34.040
Instead, it suggested an alternative: a temporary,
50-year limbo as a separate, Special Administrative

00:00:34.040 --> 00:00:36.160
Region of the PRC.

00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:42.770
This new, never-before-tried proposal raised
many questions, from the trivial to the existential:

00:00:42.770 --> 00:00:44.370
What flag would be flown?

00:00:44.370 --> 00:00:46.210
What currency would be used?

00:00:46.210 --> 00:00:48.210
Which citizenship would they hold?

00:00:48.210 --> 00:00:51.140
And how much of Britain’s presence would
remain?

00:00:51.140 --> 00:00:55.650
With no guiding historical precedent with
which to refer, as its neighbor Macau had

00:00:55.650 --> 00:01:01.160
yet to be handed over by Portugal, the answers
to these questions were wide open.

00:01:01.160 --> 00:01:08.479
Legally, only the New Territories were leased,
set to expire after 99-years in 1997.

00:01:08.479 --> 00:01:13.780
The geographically smaller but well-developed
Hong Kong Island and Kowloon Peninsula had

00:01:13.780 --> 00:01:15.880
been surrendered in perpetuity.

00:01:15.880 --> 00:01:20.659
Initially, the British considered retaining
their rightful territory to become a small,

00:01:20.659 --> 00:01:22.189
independent state.

00:01:22.189 --> 00:01:27.590
And, as proof of just how wide open the possibilities,
there was even a slightly-serious proposal

00:01:27.590 --> 00:01:31.490
to resettle its 5 million inhabitants in Northern
Ireland.

00:01:31.490 --> 00:01:36.119
Beijing, however, soon reminded Britain that
the island received its water and supplies

00:01:36.119 --> 00:01:40.490
from the mainland, whose pipes could easily
be shut off.

00:01:40.490 --> 00:01:45.900
Thus, at least one question was answered:
All of Hong Kong would become Chinese soil

00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:48.600
when the lease expired in ‘97.

00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:53.750
Of those remaining, that of citizenship - something
at the very heart of identity - proved to

00:01:53.750 --> 00:01:57.289
be especially significant and contentious.

00:01:57.289 --> 00:02:03.400
Until the 1960s, all British Nationals - whether
born on the British isles or one of its overseas

00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:09.060
colonies, like Hong Kong, were permitted to
enter and stay in the UK as full, unrestricted

00:02:09.060 --> 00:02:10.060
citizens.

00:02:10.060 --> 00:02:15.360
As its empire began unwinding in the 60s and
70s, however, it feared an influx of immigrants

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:17.940
from its African and Asian colonies.

00:02:17.940 --> 00:02:22.680
New laws were passed making it more difficult
for these groups to enter unless they or their

00:02:22.680 --> 00:02:26.080
families were, quote, ‘connected’ to the
UK.

00:02:26.080 --> 00:02:32.520
In 1981 The British Nationality Act created
three new categories: British Citizens, for

00:02:32.520 --> 00:02:39.160
residents of the UK, British Overseas Citizens,
and, for Hong Kong and other Crown colonies,

00:02:39.160 --> 00:02:41.880
British Dependent Territories Citizens.

00:02:41.880 --> 00:02:46.550
With this law, ‘British citizenship’ was
fundamentally redefined to exclude millions

00:02:46.550 --> 00:02:50.850
of overseas ‘citizens’ from the ‘right
to abode’ - the ability to enter and stay

00:02:50.850 --> 00:02:54.160
in the UK, at will, and without restriction.

00:02:54.160 --> 00:02:59.420
From then on, Hong Kongers were relegated
to a new form of literal ‘second-class citizenship’

00:02:59.420 --> 00:03:04.030
in a thinly-veiled attempt to stop non-white
immigration to the UK.

00:03:04.030 --> 00:03:08.660
And while this change was not solely-aimed
at them, it affected the territory in a very

00:03:08.660 --> 00:03:10.380
unique way.

00:03:10.380 --> 00:03:14.900
While citizens of other colonies lost their
right to abode as they gained that of their

00:03:14.900 --> 00:03:19.860
new, independent country, those in Hong Kong
were to be handed over to an existing country

00:03:19.860 --> 00:03:23.290
whose commitments had not yet been tested.

00:03:23.290 --> 00:03:28.330
In the eyes of locals, Britain had betrayed
them - a feeling which would only intensify

00:03:28.330 --> 00:03:30.390
as the years went on.

00:03:30.390 --> 00:03:34.670
Because the city would soon no longer be a
British dependent territory, a new status

00:03:34.670 --> 00:03:40.870
was created specifically for its special circumstance,
called the British National (Overseas) Passport.

00:03:40.870 --> 00:03:46.280
It, like its predecessor, lacked the right
to abode in the UK but could only be applied

00:03:46.280 --> 00:03:48.790
for before the handover.

00:03:48.790 --> 00:03:52.920
The Chinese, though, refused to accept the
embarrassing idea that Hong Kongers would

00:03:52.920 --> 00:03:57.380
remain British Nationals, of any kind, after
‘97.

00:03:57.380 --> 00:04:02.090
China insisted that, at midnight on the day
of the handover, all would lose their British

00:04:02.090 --> 00:04:05.590
status and instantly become Chinese Nationals.

00:04:05.590 --> 00:04:10.960
Their old passports could be used as travel
documents, but not as proof of British citizenship.

00:04:10.960 --> 00:04:17.120
Then, in 1989 the tanks of the People’s
Liberation Army rolled into Tiananmen Square,

00:04:17.120 --> 00:04:22.060
and the slowly-growing collective unease felt
in Hong Kong, reaching a tipping point.

00:04:22.060 --> 00:04:28.569
A million stunned citizens took to the streets,
whose fear of uncertainty had become, simply,

00:04:28.569 --> 00:04:29.569
fear.

00:04:29.569 --> 00:04:32.139
But, there was still some hope.

00:04:32.139 --> 00:04:35.710
Luckily, the British position was far from
singular.

00:04:35.710 --> 00:04:40.650
The local Hong Kong government, led by its
last governor, Chris Patten, felt this rising

00:04:40.650 --> 00:04:43.949
tension and lobbied Britain on their behalf.

00:04:43.949 --> 00:04:48.159
London expressed fear that if it gave the
BN(O) right to abode, 3 million immigrants

00:04:48.159 --> 00:04:52.099
might suddenly flood the UK, overwhelming
its social system.

00:04:52.099 --> 00:04:57.440
This, despite the fact that 30 million white
commonwealth citizens in Australia, Canada,

00:04:57.440 --> 00:05:00.550
and New Zealand also could’ve immigrated
at any given time.

00:05:00.550 --> 00:05:04.800
It would be wrong, they said, to promise the
right to abode if they could not fulfill these

00:05:04.800 --> 00:05:07.759
obligations in the event of an emergency.

00:05:07.759 --> 00:05:12.400
They also argued doing so would violate its
Joint Declaration with China, the document

00:05:12.400 --> 00:05:14.620
basis for the handover.

00:05:14.620 --> 00:05:18.520
And yet, 10 months later, Britain did just
that.

00:05:18.520 --> 00:05:23.689
The British Nationality Selection Scheme gave
full British citizenship to 50,000 hand-picked

00:05:23.689 --> 00:05:27.350
residents, along with their spouse and children
under 18.

00:05:27.350 --> 00:05:31.610
This elite group was chosen directly by the
governor and was based on four categories

00:05:31.610 --> 00:05:35.120
of people - including professionals and civil
servants.

00:05:35.120 --> 00:05:40.300
Selection was based primarily on a point system,
which rewarded, among other things, English

00:05:40.300 --> 00:05:43.680
proficiency and ‘connections’ to the UK.

00:05:43.680 --> 00:05:47.990
Those not deemed important or ‘British’
enough by their colonizers were simply out

00:05:47.990 --> 00:05:49.030
of luck.

00:05:49.030 --> 00:05:54.580
Still, knowing of these inadequacies, Patten
continued lobbying, increasingly finding himself

00:05:54.580 --> 00:05:57.580
caught between locals and officials in Britain.

00:05:57.580 --> 00:06:03.229
Ironically, both sides made the same accusation:
Locals suggested not giving the right to abode

00:06:03.229 --> 00:06:08.059
was a sign Britain must not be confident in
Hong Kong’s post-handover prospects, if

00:06:08.059 --> 00:06:10.690
it genuinely feared a mass exodus.

00:06:10.690 --> 00:06:15.370
At the same time, Britain argued that giving
the right to abode was a sign Patten wasn’t

00:06:15.370 --> 00:06:20.210
confident in his negotiations with China,
since otherwise it wouldn’t be necessary.

00:06:20.210 --> 00:06:25.930
Amidst this larger debacle, Lord Patten also
fought to resolve two unusual complications…

00:06:25.930 --> 00:06:32.139
First, Chinese Law stated that only ethnically-Chinese
Hong Kongers would automatically become Chinese

00:06:32.139 --> 00:06:34.419
Nationals at the time of the handover.

00:06:34.419 --> 00:06:41.259
But, the BN(O) passport was acquired by application,
meaning that, if one were not ethnically-Chinese

00:06:41.259 --> 00:06:46.599
and did not apply for the BN(O) passport,
at the time of the handover, their Dependent

00:06:46.599 --> 00:06:51.590
Territory Citizenship status would expire
but the Chinese would refuse to naturalize

00:06:51.590 --> 00:06:55.169
them, leaving them, effectively, stateless.

00:06:55.169 --> 00:07:00.099
One Indian family, in particular, had lived
in British Hong Kong for 60-years, received

00:07:00.099 --> 00:07:05.339
British educations, spoke with British accents,
and yet, only some were granted full British

00:07:05.339 --> 00:07:09.220
citizenship while their brothers and sisters
were not.

00:07:09.220 --> 00:07:13.160
Even after learning of these circumstances,
the British Home Office held the position

00:07:13.160 --> 00:07:18.219
for years that they would simply become British
Overseas Citizens and that if they were indeed

00:07:18.219 --> 00:07:22.939
forced out of Hong Kong, Britain would then
consider them for immigration.

00:07:22.939 --> 00:07:27.600
Only in the last year before the handover
did Britain finally acquiesce.

00:07:27.600 --> 00:07:30.819
Second, was the question of visas.

00:07:30.819 --> 00:07:35.960
A new Special Administrative Region passport
would be created for after the handover, featuring

00:07:35.960 --> 00:07:40.870
the same national emblem and illustration
of the Great Wall as that of the mainland.

00:07:40.870 --> 00:07:45.199
The question was whether its holders would
be given visa-free access to the UK.

00:07:45.199 --> 00:07:50.509
Here, too, the British Home Office pushed
back, suggesting anti-immigration voters would

00:07:50.509 --> 00:07:53.949
make no distinction between the right to abode
and visa-free access.

00:07:53.949 --> 00:07:59.490
Patten, in turn, countered that not doing
so would infuriate the Chinese, who would

00:07:59.490 --> 00:08:04.219
lobby for fewer rights for the BN(O) passport,
making it even less valuable.

00:08:04.219 --> 00:08:11.009
Hong Kong would also likely impose visa restrictions
on its 400,000 annual British visitors.

00:08:11.009 --> 00:08:15.460
After much back-and-forth, Beijing assured
them that the SAR passport would only be available

00:08:15.460 --> 00:08:22.009
to holders of permanent Hong Kong ID cards,
only in Hong Kong, and only by Hong Kong immigration

00:08:22.009 --> 00:08:23.759
authorities.

00:08:23.759 --> 00:08:28.389
6-month visa-free access was secured, and
the Prime Minister announced “We in Britain

00:08:28.389 --> 00:08:32.590
will have continuing responsibilities to the
people of Hong Kong…[and] will be watching

00:08:32.590 --> 00:08:37.570
to see that the letter and spirit of the Joint
Declaration are honoured, now, next year and

00:08:37.570 --> 00:08:39.150
for fifty years beyond.”

00:08:39.150 --> 00:08:45.009
- words deliberately chosen to prevent any
future British politician from reneging, or,

00:08:45.009 --> 00:08:48.339
at least, to make a fool of anyone who tried.

00:08:48.339 --> 00:08:54.029
These two rare successes in the long, drawn-out
leadup to the handover were celebrated but

00:08:54.029 --> 00:08:59.570
still left what many considered the largest
source of unease: the status of the BN(O)

00:08:59.570 --> 00:09:00.570
passport.

00:09:00.570 --> 00:09:05.279
This tension became palpable in the final
months before the handover as tens of thousands

00:09:05.279 --> 00:09:11.840
stood in line to register for the British
passport 24-hours before the deadline.

00:09:11.840 --> 00:09:13.269
Finally, the day came.

00:09:13.269 --> 00:09:20.500
On July 1st, 1997, after nearly two decades
of negotiations, vague promises, worry, and

00:09:20.500 --> 00:09:24.140
skepticism, Hong Kong became China.

00:09:24.140 --> 00:09:30.220
Citizenship, however, always remained contentious,
and, in the eyes of many, an unforgivable

00:09:30.220 --> 00:09:32.810
black mark on Britain’s legacy.

00:09:32.810 --> 00:09:37.200
It has even since been revealed that Britain
asked Portugal not to give full citizenship

00:09:37.200 --> 00:09:41.880
to Macau residents, which would make it look
bad in comparison.

00:09:41.880 --> 00:09:46.160
Some renounced their British citizenship in
a pledge of support to China.

00:09:46.160 --> 00:09:50.520
Others lost credibility for holding foreign
passports, which some said undermined their

00:09:50.520 --> 00:09:52.570
claims of loyalty.

00:09:52.570 --> 00:09:57.889
Over time, the BN(O) passport, once seen as
a valuable insurance policy, became more of

00:09:57.889 --> 00:10:00.170
an expensive piece of paper.

00:10:00.170 --> 00:10:06.079
Compared to the Hong Kong SAR passport, it’s
three times as costly, offers 48 fewer visa-free

00:10:06.079 --> 00:10:11.380
countries and territories, and, perhaps most
embarrassing, is less useful when entering

00:10:11.380 --> 00:10:12.730
the UK.

00:10:12.730 --> 00:10:17.240
While Hong Kong passports allow expedited
immigration, holders of the BN(O) fill out

00:10:17.240 --> 00:10:20.820
landing cards and enter the UK like any other
foreigner.

00:10:20.820 --> 00:10:25.199
They also can’t apply for Australian visas
online and are banned from becoming Chief

00:10:25.199 --> 00:10:26.680
Executive.

00:10:26.680 --> 00:10:31.050
Most of its holders will never have use for
its few benefits like the right to join the

00:10:31.050 --> 00:10:34.569
British armed forces and get married under
British law.

00:10:34.569 --> 00:10:40.490
And, as one final nail in the coffin, because
China views the BN(O) as merely a travel document,

00:10:40.490 --> 00:10:45.790
it does not allow its holders British consular
access anywhere on Chinese soil - whether

00:10:45.790 --> 00:10:48.540
on the mainland, Macau, or Hong Kong.

00:10:48.540 --> 00:10:53.760
In practice, the BN(O) is similar to the status
of those born in American Samoa to non-U.S.

00:10:53.760 --> 00:10:55.579
citizen parents.

00:10:55.579 --> 00:11:00.889
Despite being on American soil, such a person
is regarded as a U.S. National but not a U.S.

00:11:00.889 --> 00:11:05.540
Citizen - and thus not allowed to vote, serve
on a jury, or apply for restricted government

00:11:05.540 --> 00:11:06.540
jobs.

00:11:06.540 --> 00:11:11.900
Although, unlike BN(O) holders, they do have
the right to reside in any part of the country.

00:11:11.900 --> 00:11:15.180
That is, until, possibly, now.

00:11:15.180 --> 00:11:19.940
After Beijing announced a National Security
Bill which will outlaw “secession, subversion,

00:11:19.940 --> 00:11:25.089
and terrorism” against the Chinese National
government in Hong Kong, Britain responded

00:11:25.089 --> 00:11:30.470
that it’s now, 23 years later, ‘reconsidering’
the rights of BN(O) passports.

00:11:30.470 --> 00:11:35.940
So far, it’s offered to extend visa-free
stays from six to extendable-periods of twelve

00:11:35.940 --> 00:11:41.019
months, allowing for work and study, and creating
a path for citizenship.

00:11:41.019 --> 00:11:46.079
Although this applies to all roughly 3-million
holders of the passport, whether or not they’re

00:11:46.079 --> 00:11:51.480
currently renewed, as well as their dependents,
it leaves out the majority of its population.

00:11:51.480 --> 00:11:56.759
Given that the BN(O) could only be applied
for before 1997 and can’t be passed down,

00:11:56.759 --> 00:12:03.170
those under 23 - a large swath of Hong Kong’s
protestors - will likely be ineligible.

00:12:03.170 --> 00:12:07.730
Many details are yet unknown: Will these rights
be extended to all residents?

00:12:07.730 --> 00:12:11.550
What, exactly, will be the criteria for permanent
citizenship?

00:12:11.550 --> 00:12:13.960
And how will China retaliate?

00:12:13.960 --> 00:12:18.899
In the absence of any answers to these questions,
many see Britain’s offer as more political

00:12:18.899 --> 00:12:19.899
than practical.

00:12:19.899 --> 00:12:25.959
In truth, the right to abode was always misunderstood
as a debate about immigration, when, in fact,

00:12:25.959 --> 00:12:28.110
it was one of confidence.

00:12:28.110 --> 00:12:33.060
Rather than encourage migration, it was always
intended to prevent it.

00:12:33.060 --> 00:12:37.120
Patten knew the health of Hong Kong’s bureaucracy
would be left in the hands of a relatively

00:12:37.120 --> 00:12:40.950
small number of highly-educated, well-off
individuals.

00:12:40.950 --> 00:12:45.279
The right to abode was about instilling the
confidence necessary to keep them in Hong

00:12:45.279 --> 00:12:49.509
Kong after the handover - along with their
democratic ideals.

00:12:49.509 --> 00:12:53.880
It would also act as a check on Beijing - the
knowledge that it may lose the city’s most

00:12:53.880 --> 00:12:56.529
talented if it did not keep them satisfied.

00:12:56.529 --> 00:13:01.500
Indeed, famously, in the years before the
handover, hundreds of thousands of Hong Kongers

00:13:01.500 --> 00:13:06.509
fled to Canada, Australia, the U.S., U.K.,
and elsewhere.

00:13:06.509 --> 00:13:09.379
Their reasons, however, are largely misunderstood.

00:13:09.379 --> 00:13:14.970
Some, of course, stayed abroad - where they
established Cantonese as the language of Chinatowns.

00:13:14.970 --> 00:13:19.620
Many, on the other hand, stayed only long
enough to gain citizenship, before returning

00:13:19.620 --> 00:13:22.510
to Hong Kong with this new insurance plan.

00:13:22.510 --> 00:13:27.319
This is so common, in fact, it has a name:
“The return tide of Hong Kong”.

00:13:27.319 --> 00:13:32.160
An estimated 35% of those who left in the
80s returned later.

00:13:32.160 --> 00:13:37.029
Another - ‘Astronaut’ refers to one family
member - usually the father - who stayed,

00:13:37.029 --> 00:13:41.709
making money in Hong Kong while the rest of
the family lived abroad - in Vancouver, Sydney,

00:13:41.709 --> 00:13:45.740
San Francisco, or elsewhere - to gain citizenship.

00:13:45.740 --> 00:13:51.060
Such is the predicament of Hong Kongers - they
love their city, their language, and their

00:13:51.060 --> 00:13:52.120
way of life.

00:13:52.120 --> 00:13:56.600
They want, above all else, and as long as
possible, to stay.

00:13:56.600 --> 00:14:01.259
And yet, they also know these things are temporary.

00:14:01.259 --> 00:14:07.110
Now, just as foreign passports act as a sort
of insurance plan for Hong Kongers, a strong

00:14:07.110 --> 00:14:12.389
understanding of math, science, and especially
computer science, is the universal insurance

00:14:12.389 --> 00:14:17.199
plan that can help you earn better grades,
get better jobs, and ride out recessions like

00:14:17.199 --> 00:14:19.000
the one we’re currently in.

00:14:19.000 --> 00:14:23.230
